    }
  }
  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining CariCloud\'s Structure**\n\nI\'m solidifying the foundation for CariCloud, beginning with its visual identity. I\'ve landed on \"CariCloud\" as the name, designed for a point-of-sale system tailored for local carinderias in Marikina City, Philippines. The primary accent color will be #E65100. The color palette represents the warmth and vitality inherent in carinderias.\n\n\n**Refining Visual Palette**\n\nI\'ve decided on some more specific colors. I am settling on `#FF6D00` and `#F57C00` for secondary accents and `#FAF8F5`, `#F5F2EC`, and slate grays for neutrals. The aim is a balance of warmth and readability. The system will feature large, high-contrast touch targets, which are critical for busy environments.\n\nI\'m thinking of a theme customization feature as a Tier 2 add-on. Initial themes would include the default Marikina Warm Orange, Clean Emerald Green, Modern Slate, and a Dark Mode option.\n\nI\'m starting to think about core architecture. It will be a web app with IndexedDB for offline persistence. There will be a visual sync indicator, and buttons for manual sync and background re-sync.\n\n\n**Defining User Roles**\n\nI\'m now focusing on user roles. I\'ve designed an admin vs. staff mode, using a PIN-based login. The cashier will use a quick POS, while the admin gets menu management, tax tracking, and audit tools. I am now refining these concepts into an actionable structure.\n\nI\'m beginning to map out detailed module specifications for CariCloud.\n\n\n**Designing POS Functionality**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in the practical aspects of CariCloud\'s Point of Sale module. The \"Sold Out\" badges will provide an easy manual toggle for menu items. I\'m focusing on high-speed order flow, with a strict 3-tap checkout system for peak hours. Additionally, I\'m working on statutory discount processing for Senior Citizen and PWD customers, and I am incorporating multi-modal payment settlement.\n\n\n**Implementing Credit & Analytics**\n\nI\'m now integrating an asynchronous webhook for payment verification, featuring an \"Order Complete\" instant receipt closure. Next, I\'m developing a credit system with a \"Listahan\" ledger, requiring admin approval for exceeding credit limits via a PIN. I\'m building an end-of-day reconciliation module that tracks cash counts, food waste, and calculates profits. The debt ledger will display a digital customer credit list, with payment logging and tax relief tracking.\n\n\n**Calculating Tax Relief**\n\nI\'m now integrating Marikina City\'s 2026 tax relief policy. I\'m focusing on features like a visual progress bar indicating gross sales towards the \342\202\261250,000 threshold, along with eligibility status badges. I\'m also including automated BPLO reporting, generating gross sales declaration forms. A tiered subscription model is also under consideration.\n\n\n**Defining Subscription Tiers**\n\nI\'m now concretely defining the subscription tiers for CariCloud. Tier 2 at \342\202\26199/mo will allow custom UI themes and dashboard personalization. Tier 3 at \342\202\261199/mo adds Marikina tax relief tracking and BPLO declaration form automation. I\'m focusing on building an interactive subscription settings panel that will allow store owners to simulate tiers, test features, and easily upgrade subscriptions.\n\nI am also defining operational boundaries, it is only for internal use, no AI and no HR.\nI am planning on the `/src` file directory structure.\n\n\n**Structuring Initial Data**\n\nI\'m now populating sample data including menu items (Adobo, Sinigang, etc.), suki customer records with credit, transactions, and EOD logs. This will provide a solid foundation for testing.\n\nI\'m also setting up the backend structure. I\'m focusing on `/src/services/db.ts`, for client-side storage management with IndexedDB wrapper. And `/src/services/api.ts`, which will be the Express backend client API route wrapper. I\'ve got the Express backend server at `/server.ts` that will be handling various API endpoints.\nI am also starting to define the `components`.\n\n\n\n**Defining Module Layouts**\n\nI\'m now laying out the module components: `ReceiptModal.tsx` for printable receipts, `MenuManagementModule.tsx` for daily menu control, `EODModule.tsx` for end-of-day financial reconciliation, `ListahanModule.tsx` for customer credit tracking, `BploTaxModule.tsx` for tax relief, `SubscriptionModule.tsx` for subscription tier selection, and `SettingsModule.tsx` for operator mode and staff PIN management. I\'m focusing on clean, intuitive layouts with an easy user interface.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 16
      nanos: 579979568
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
