  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Constructing the Modal Design**\n\nI\'m currently fleshing out the JSX for the \"Create Account\" modal. I\'m focusing on its structure, placing it right before the footer, making it fixed and covering the screen with a backdrop blur. My goal is a visually appealing and user-friendly modal experience. I\'m making sure it\'s properly layered with a z-index of 50.\n\n\n**Refining the Modal Structure**\n\nI\'ve been working on the styling, specifically focusing on the new account modal\'s layout. I\'m leveraging Tailwind classes like `justify-between` and `space-x-3` to refine the header and overall appearance, including the rounded corners and shadow effects. I\'m also addressing the visual hierarchy with a distinct border and spacing. Finally, I\'m ensuring it\'s responsive with a maximum width and height.\n\n\n**Implementing the Close Button**\n\nI\'ve just added the close button functionality to the \"Create Account\" modal, making sure it visually fits in with the design. It\'s a small button, but it\'s crucial for the user experience! I\'ve styled it as a small \"X\" using Tailwind classes to maintain the desired look and feel.\n\n\n**Defining User Roles**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the user role selection within the new account modal, implementing distinct buttons for store owners and employees. I\'ve integrated visual cues, using Tailwind, to clearly indicate the selected role, including background changes, shadow effects, and appropriate icons. I am refining the button styling and adding logic for their functionality.\n\n\n**Adding Input Fields**\n\nI\'m now integrating the input fields for full name and email within the new account modal. I\'m focusing on their styling to match the existing design, using Tailwind classes for consistent borders, rounded corners, and focus effects. The placeholder text dynamically changes based on the selected user role, and both fields will be required.\n\n\n**Adding Password Fields**\n\nI\'m now integrating the password and confirm password input fields within the modal. These will also have the same styling to ensure consistency with existing fields, and use Tailwind for visual cues and error states. They will be required fields. I\'ve chosen `type=\"password\"` for both and using placeholders for visual feedback to the user.\n\n\n**Adding the PIN Field**\n\nI\'m now integrating the PIN code input field within the new account modal. It will be styled consistently with other fields, maintaining borders, rounded corners, and focus effects using Tailwind. This PIN will be required. I\'ve been focusing on consistency for a clean visual experience.\n\n\n**Validating the PIN**\n\nI\'ve just added error handling for the PIN input. I\'ve included a real-time validation check for the 4-digit security PIN to ensure it\'s a valid number, which means I\'ve used `maxLength={4}` and other validation logic. I\'ve also incorporated an error banner that appears if the input is incorrect. This is an important security and usability feature. I\'m focusing on providing clear and concise feedback to the user.\n\n\n**Finalizing the Modal Buttons**\n\nI\'ve just styled the modal\'s action buttons. I\'ve got a \"Cancel\" button styled with subtle hover effects, maintaining the current color scheme. I\'ve also created a \"Create Account\" button using Tailwind, with an orange background, white text, and an icon, to stand out. I\'m focusing on consistency in this final layout. I\'ve also added a \"Create an Account\" button option to the landing portal choice screen.\n\n\n"
